[?2004l[?2004h> Problem:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------
[?2004l[?2004h> The script was flagging ~4000 requests as slow when only ~200 were actually slow.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Root Cause:
[?2004l[?2004h> -----------
[?2004l[?2004h> The regex pattern 'response_time=(\d+)ms' was matching 'upstream_response_time=XXXms'
[?2004l[?2004h> because it didn't prevent substring matches. The pattern matched the substring
[?2004l[?2004h> 'response_time=XXXms' within 'upstream_response_time=XXXms'.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Additionally, the production logs use TWO formats:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. response_time=XXXms (with ms suffix)
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. response_time_ms=XXX (without ms suffix)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The original pattern only handled format #1.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Solution:
[?2004l[?2004h> ---------
[?2004l[?2004h> Updated the regex patterns to:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Use negative lookbehind: (?<!upstream_)response_time=(\d+)ms
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Handle both formats with two patterns
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. This prevents matching upstream_response_time while catching both formats
[?2004l[?2004h> 
